All 7 tests pass: `VERIFY_TASK6: PASS`. Only `lib/paginate.js` was edited; tests untouched.

The three off-by-one bugs, and the fix:

| Bug | Fix |
|---|---|
| `start = (page-1)*perPage + 1` — skips item 1 on page 1, so page 1 was ids 2–11 and page 2 started on the last item of page 1 | `start = (page-1)*perPage` (page 1 starts at offset 0) |
| `end = Math.min(start+perPage+1, len)` then `slice(start-1, end-1)` — mixed exclusive/inclusive logic, inflating each page | `end = start + perPage`, used directly as the exclusive slice bound, clamped by `Math.min` |
| `hasPrev = page > 0` — true for page 1, where there is no previous page | `hasPrev = page > 1` |

One guard beyond the arithmetic, driven by the contract's out-of-range clause: `inRange = page >= 1 && start < source.length`, so an out-of-range page yields `items: []` and `hasNext: false`. Without it, a negative page would slice from the *end* of the array (a negative `start` in `slice` counts from the end) and `hasNext` would compute `true` from a negative `end` — i.e. contract violations that the tests don't happen to exercise.

Final file (`lib/paginate.js:12-30`):

```js
export function paginate(items, { page = 1, perPage = 10, sortKey = null } = {}) {
  const source = sortKey ? [...items].sort((a, b) => a[sortKey] - b[sortKey]) : items;

  // page is 1-indexed, so page 1 starts at offset 0; slice's end is exclusive.
  const start = (page - 1) * perPage;
  const end = start + perPage;
  const inRange = page >= 1 && start < source.length;

  return {
    items: inRange ? source.slice(start, Math.min(end, source.length)) : [],
    page,
    perPage,
    total: source.length,
    hasPrev: page > 1,
    hasNext: inRange && end < source.length,
  };
}
```

Two notes I deliberately left alone, since they're outside the off-by-one scope and no test covers them:

- **`sortKey` with string values**: `a[sortKey] - b[sortKey]` yields `NaN`, so a comparator that returns `NaN` leaves the array untouched — `sortKey: "name"` silently sorts nothing. A `<`/`>` comparator would fix it in one line; say the word and I'll add it.
- **Mutation**: `sortKey` sorts a copy, and the non-sorting path returns the caller's own array by reference (`items` is the same object in the result), so a consumer can mutate the source through `out.items`. Not a contract violation today.
